1
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:02.933
How you handle

2
00:00:02.933 --> 00:00:07.933
Personally Identifiable Information,
or PII, is a critical concern

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.200
when building any application.
But it's especially important in

4
00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:12.733
genAI

5
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:15.733
applications that use a third-party model

6
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.800
or where you retrieve text from
lots of diverse internal documents.

7
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.300
In this lesson, you'll build a validator
to check your user prompts for any PII,

8
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:29.400
and if there's PII present,
then prevent it from being passed

9
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.533
over the internet to a third party
model provider.

10
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:37.200
You'll also use a state-of-the-art
validator from Guardrails Hub to

11
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.800
check LLM outputs for PII and redacted

12
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.600
before it's shown to the user.

13
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.300
Let's dive in.

14
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.633
PII in general,

15
00:00:45.633 --> 00:00:47.833
you know, is something
that should be taken pretty seriously.

16
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:49.133
PII stands for personal

17
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:53.200
identifiable information such as names,
emails, social security numbers,

18
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.400
anything that might be sensitive
and could be used to identify you.

19
00:00:57.833 --> 00:00:58.633
This is a pretty

20
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:02.100
important risk
that's associated with using LLMs.

21
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.833
And getting your customers to use LLMs.

22
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:08.833
Since most developers and organizations
that use LLMs use

23
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.100
LLMs that are developed by third-party
propriety organizations,

24
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.100
and so you have to make this external
API request that sends over all of,

25
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.400
you know, maybe your data,
maybe your customer data, etc...

26
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.900
So essentially, what you want to do when

27
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.833
being mindful of PII leakage is,
first of all, making sure

28
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:31.433
that none of your customers

29
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:35.100
data or your employee's data
or any of your organization's private data

30
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.000
is ever leaked out to the third party LLM
provider.

31
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:44.700
The second thing to be mindful of
is to make sure that in your responses

32
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.800
to your users, that there's
none of your own organization's data

33
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.033
that is accidentally sent to somebody
that should not be seeing it

34
00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:54.500
via the LLM responses.

35
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.500
So for this lesson,
we're actually going to be

36
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.400
using a really great open source project
by Microsoft.

37
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.600
It's called Microsoft Presidio,
and it's essentially a

38
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.600
tool that helps analyze and anonymize

39
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.700
many, many different types of PII.

40
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.233
And we're going to dig into more details
about how to use later in the lesson.

41
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
I'm going to start
with our usual practices here

42
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.000
where we're going to copy over our warning
filters.

43
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
We are also going to copy over our imports
that we need for our lesson.

